<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Uganda Martyrs</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Martyrs of Uganda</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Uganda Martyrs were Christian converts, Catholic and Anglican, killed on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II of Buganda</a> between 1885 and 1887, many of them young pages at the royal court. The largest group was burned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namugongo">Namugongo</a> on 3 June 1886. Pope Paul VI canonised twenty-two Catholic martyrs, led by Charles Lwanga, in 1964, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Anglican Church</a> also commemorates its martyrs.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Alexander Mackay of the Anglican Church Missionary Society reached Buganda in 1877, and French Catholic White Fathers led by Simon Lourdel followed two years later; Arab traders from Zanzibar had already brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. The three faiths competed for influence at the royal court, and converts of each held important posts. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwanga-ii">Mwanga II</a>, who became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> in 1884, was concerned at the growing influence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and at a new class of educated, reform-minded officials. On 29 October 1885 he had the incoming Anglican bishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-hannington">James Hannington</a> killed on the eastern border of the kingdom.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Joseph Mukasa, a convert who had deplored Hannington's killing and tried to protect the court pages, was executed on 15 November 1885 at the instigation of the Katikkiro, or prime minister. Between 25 May and 3 June 1886 a wider series of executions followed, as Mwanga ordered the killing of the young men who disobeyed him. Most were between 15 and 30 years old, and many were taken on a long journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namugongo">Namugongo</a> to be burned alive.</p><p>Historians still debate the causes. Lourdel, a witness, held that Mwanga felt despised by literate Christians, and gave as a second cause the pages' refusal of the king's sexual demands; Marie de Kiewet-Hemphill and J. P. Thoonen see that refusal as the immediate pretext. Others stress politics: Ward argued that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabaka-of-buganda">Kabaka</a> saw the Christians as rebels and tools of foreign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> the deaths were taken up in a campaign for intervention, and the Church Missionary Society later used them to win public support for acquiring Uganda; on 27 August 1894 Mwanga accepted Buganda becoming a British protectorate. Pope Benedict XV beatified Charles Lwanga, Matiya Mulumba and their twenty companions on 6 June 1920. At the canonisation of 18 October 1964, Pope Paul VI also named the Anglicans who had died. The Basilica of the Uganda Martyrs at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namugongo">Namugongo</a>, built in 1968, has drawn great pilgrimages since the 1980s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-england">Church of England</a> remembers the 23 Anglican martyrs on 3 June.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Uganda Martyrs” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The execution of young Christian converts on the orders of Kabaka Mwanga II in 1885–1887, the <b>Uganda Martyrs</b>, many of whom died at Namugongo in June 1886, gave Uganda some of Africa’s best-known saints, canonised by the Catholic Church in 1964.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-buganda">Kingdom of Buganda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
